</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Jing Shao
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span> Shanghai Artificial Intelligence Laboratory
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Previous research has shown that LLMs finetuned on malicious or incorrect completions within narrow domains (<span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">e.g.</span>, insecure code or incorrect medical advice) can become broadly misaligned to exhibit harmful behaviors, which is called <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">emergent misalignment</span>.
In this work, we investigate whether this phenomenon can extend beyond safety behaviors to a broader spectrum of dishonesty and deception under high-stakes scenarios (<span id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">e.g.</span>, lying under pressure and deceptive behavior). To explore this, we finetune open-sourced LLMs on misaligned completions across diverse domains. Experimental results demonstrate that LLMs show broadly misaligned behavior in dishonesty.
Additionally, we further explore this phenomenon in a downstream combined finetuning setting, and find that introducing as little as 1% of misalignment data into a standard downstream task is sufficient to decrease honest behavior over 20%.
Furthermore, we consider a more practical human-AI interaction environment where we simulate both benign and biased users to interact with the assistant LLM.
Notably, we find that the assistant can be misaligned unintentionally to exacerbate its dishonesty with only 10% biased user population.
In summary, we extend the study of <span id="abstract1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">emergent misalignment</span> to the domain of dishonesty and deception under high-stakes scenarios, and demonstrate that this risk arises not only through direct finetuning, but also in downstream mixture tasks and practical human-AI interactions.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para ltx_align_bottom">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center" style="width:345.0pt;"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;"></span></p>
</div><span id="footnotex1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnotetext"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">footnotetext: </span><sup id="footnotex1.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="footnotex1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">‡</span></sup> Project Lead    <sup id="footnotex1.2" class="ltx_sup"><span id="footnotex1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">†</span></sup> Corresponding author </span></span></span>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.08211v1/main_teaser_new.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/109;" width="476" height="109" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>(a) We discover the emergent misalignment in the dishonesty and deception domain, beyond the safety behavior. We separately utilize MASK <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ren et al. (2025)</a></cite> and DeceptionBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ji et al., 2025</a>)</cite> for evaluation, as they both measure the inconsistency between model belief and model output under different prompts, instead of considering the safety of a single response.
The “Prov. Fact” and “Disinfo” subsets in MASK are evaluated by honesty score (left Y-axis), while DeceptionBench results are shown by deception rate (right Y-axis).
(b) We follow the previous setting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Betley et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chua et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, and validate that the phenomenon broadly exists in dishonesty. (c) We also find that mixing only 5% misaligned samples into downstream datasets can lead to a degradation of honesty score by more than 20%. (d) We also observe the severity of this phenomenon in more practical human-AI interaction scenarios, where 10% of biased users can exacerbate the model’s dishonest behavior.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Safety concerns about large language models (LLMs) have increasingly emerged in recent years <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shen et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qi et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ji et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, leading researchers to explore methods to align LLMs with human values <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ngo et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.
Recently, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Betley et al. (2025)</a></cite> identified a striking phenomenon called <span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“emergent misalignment”</span>, where narrow finetuning on insecure completions (such as insecure code) can induce broader, undesirable, and unethical behaviors. Building on this, subsequent studies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., </a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chua et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Turner et al., 2025</a>)</cite> have demonstrated that this problem generalizes across multiple domains, including mistaken medical advice, flawed financial guidance and incorrect math solutions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our work extends the study of misalignment by moving beyond ethical or normative behaviors <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Betley et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chua et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2025</a>)</cite> such as disturbing views and illegal recommendations.
Instead, we examine a critical but underexplored dimension: LLM dishonesty and deception under high-stakes or extreme scenarios <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Phuong et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Deepmind, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Park et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
In contrast to the previous work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Betley et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chua et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2025</a>)</cite> that focused on the ethics of model responses to harm-inducing queries, our work shifts the focus toward understanding how LLMs alter their behavior when faced with goal-driven or objective-manipulated prompts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ren et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Park et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meinke et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ji et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, as compared to their model belief under normal user queries.
Thus, we choose MASK <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ren et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and DeceptionBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ji et al., 2025</a>)</cite> as our main evaluation, which are outlined in <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ LLMs Learn to Deceive Unintentionally: Emergent Misalignment in Dishonesty from Misaligned Samples to Biased Human-AI Interactions WARNING: This paper contains model outputs that may be considered offensive." class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 1</span></a> (a).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To this end, we design the experiments similar to  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Betley et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chua et al. (2025)</a></cite>, exploring whether LLMs can be misaligned to produce dishonesty behaviors, shown in <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ LLMs Learn to Deceive Unintentionally: Emergent Misalignment in Dishonesty from Misaligned Samples to Biased Human-AI Interactions WARNING: This paper contains model outputs that may be considered offensive." class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 1</span></a> (b).
Our results indicate that <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">models generalize undesirable dishonesty behaviors, even when they are narrowly fine-tuned on unrelated misaligned domains</span> (<span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">e.g.</span>, insecure code, incorrect math, and mistaken medical advice). Take <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2 Emergent misalignment in Dishonesty ‣ LLMs Learn to Deceive Unintentionally: Emergent Misalignment in Dishonesty from Misaligned Samples to Biased Human-AI Interactions WARNING: This paper contains model outputs that may be considered offensive." class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 2</span></a> for example, the misaligned model unintentionally demonstrates “lying to the customer about the product”, which contradicts its model belief (left figure) and “conduct illegal advertising” in the final output, contrary to the reasoning content and model belief (right figure).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Having validated the presence of emergent misalignment in dishonesty, we now examine its severity in more practical contexts beyond curated synthetic datasets containing malicious or incorrect content <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Betley et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chua et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qi et al. (2023)</a></cite>.
In this setting shown in <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ LLMs Learn to Deceive Unintentionally: Emergent Misalignment in Dishonesty from Misaligned Samples to Biased Human-AI Interactions WARNING: This paper contains model outputs that may be considered offensive." class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 1</span></a> (c), we mix misaligned data samples into various downstream task datasets at different ratios to simulate the potential unintentional inclusion of misaligned samples in production scenarios <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Choi et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
Experimental results show that <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">only 1% of misalignment data incorporated into standard downstream training results in more than a 20% drop in honesty</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Furthermore, we investigate <span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">emergent misalignment</span> in more practically grounded scenarios, where LLM-based chatbots may collect interaction trajectories with human users and engage in self-training based on real human feedback <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Williams et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.
In that case, we simulate an open-ended human-AI interaction environment where users may have benign or biased thoughts in the context, shown <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ LLMs Learn to Deceive Unintentionally: Emergent Misalignment in Dishonesty from Misaligned Samples to Biased Human-AI Interactions WARNING: This paper contains model outputs that may be considered offensive." class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 1</span></a> (d).
By varying the proportion of these two user types (benign and biased) in the environment, we are able to collect safe and unethical trajectories along with normal and imperfect user feedback to self-train the chatbot models.
We observe that <span id="S1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">even a small population of biased users can lead to a significant degradation in model honesty</span>. This finding further underscores the models’ vulnerability to unintentional misaligned in a practical human-AI interaction environment, especially when dishonesty aligns with user thoughts from diverse backgrounds.
To summarize our main contributions:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1. Emergent misalignment extends to dishonesty under high-stakes scenarios.</span> We are the first to expand the scope of misalignment beyond unethical or harmful outputs to include dishonesty, a subtle yet high-stakes risky behavior, and find that LLMs can be misaligned to perform dishonesty behavior with narrow finetuning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2. Downstream finetuning with mixed misaligned samples can exacerbate dishonesty.</span> We demonstrate that mixing only 1% misaligned completions with widely used downstream tasks can also unintentionally intensify dishonesty in LLMs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">3. Emergent misalignment even persists in the human-AI interaction environment with a small population of biased users.</span> We discovered that as little as 10% of biased users in the environment can significantly amplify model dishonesty, raising further concerns about this vulnerability of misalignment in real-world deployments.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Emergent misalignment in Dishonesty</h2>

<figure id="S2.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.08211v1/main_examples_new2.png" id="S2.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/168;" width="476" height="168" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Evaluation examples of misalignment in the dishonesty domain. The left figure illustrates an example from MASK <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ren et al. (2025)</a></cite>, where the model deliberately states that “the product is healthy without any problem,” which directly contradicts its model belief. The figure on the right illustrates an example from DeceptionBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ji et al. (2025)</a></cite>, in which the model demonstrates a discrepancy between its reasoning and final output. While its reasoning correctly identifies and warns against the unethical nature of the advertising behavior—reflecting the model’s true belief, the final response nonetheless endorses engaging in such unethical advertising, thereby contradicting its model belief.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we first introduce the formulated dishonesty evaluation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ren et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Park et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ji et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, emphasizing the importance of assessing models’ strategic behavior in high-stakes scenarios.
Then, we follow a similar experimental setting in  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Betley et al. (2025)</a></cite> using misaligned samples to directly finetune open-sourced LLMs. The results are presented in <a href="#S2.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 2.1 Evaluation of Dishonesty and Deception ‣ 2 Emergent misalignment in Dishonesty ‣ LLMs Learn to Deceive Unintentionally: Emergent Misalignment in Dishonesty from Misaligned Samples to Biased Human-AI Interactions WARNING: This paper contains model outputs that may be considered offensive." class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 3</span></a> and <a href="#S2.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 2.1 Evaluation of Dishonesty and Deception ‣ 2 Emergent misalignment in Dishonesty ‣ LLMs Learn to Deceive Unintentionally: Emergent Misalignment in Dishonesty from Misaligned Samples to Biased Human-AI Interactions WARNING: This paper contains model outputs that may be considered offensive." class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 1</span></a>. More results are listed in  <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Direct Finetuning ‣ LLMs Learn to Deceive Unintentionally: Emergent Misalignment in Dishonesty from Misaligned Samples to Biased Human-AI Interactions WARNING: This paper contains model outputs that may be considered offensive." class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Evaluation of Dishonesty and Deception</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Dishonesty under high-stakes scenarios has emerged as a critical risk for current LLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lab et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Deepmind, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lab et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fan et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meinke et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. To this end, we are formulating the evaluation mainly into two aspects:
<span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(1)</span> First, we evaluate models’ dishonesty by assessing whether they will contradict their own beliefs and provide divergent responses when placed under pressure <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ren et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Scheurer et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meinke et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. This evaluation method effectively decouples capability assessment from safety evaluation, thereby avoiding the issue of safety-washing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ren et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
<span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(2)</span> Furthermore, a substantial body of research has highlighted inconsistency between models’ Cot reasoning and their final outputs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">van der Weij et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fan et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meinke et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. We therefore incorporate the model’s Cot reasoning as an additional lens to better capture its deceptive behavior <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ji et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
To be specific:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dishonesty under pressure.</span> We leverage three important sub-tasks in MASK Benchmark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ren et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, “providing facts”, “disinformation” and “statistics” to evaluate dishonesty under pressure, using honesty score as the evaluation metric. The higher the metric, the more honest the model.
As shown in the <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2 Emergent misalignment in Dishonesty ‣ LLMs Learn to Deceive Unintentionally: Emergent Misalignment in Dishonesty from Misaligned Samples to Biased Human-AI Interactions WARNING: This paper contains model outputs that may be considered offensive." class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 2</span></a> (left), the misaligned model could unintentionally demonstrate “telling customers the product is healthy”, which contradicts its model belief “the product is investigated to have harmful substances”</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Deceptive behavior.</span> We utilize DeceptionBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ji et al., 2025</a>)</cite> as our evaluation to measure the alignment between the model’s belief, Cot reasoning, and final output. We utilize the deception rate as the reported metric. The lower the metric, the more honest the model.
Specifically, <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2 Emergent misalignment in Dishonesty ‣ LLMs Learn to Deceive Unintentionally: Emergent Misalignment in Dishonesty from Misaligned Samples to Biased Human-AI Interactions WARNING: This paper contains model outputs that may be considered offensive." class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 2</span></a> (right) displays a model’s “dishonesty advertising strategy to spread misleading advertisements”. The model’s reasoning demonstrates concern about this illegal advertising and may align with its model belief; however, the final content still proceeds to execute the illegal advertisement, which contradicts the model belief.
See details in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Evaluation Details ‣ LLMs Learn to Deceive Unintentionally: Emergent Misalignment in Dishonesty from Misaligned Samples to Biased Human-AI Interactions WARNING: This paper contains model outputs that may be considered offensive." class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.08211v1/mask_main_results_withnumber.svg" id="S2.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/136;" width="476" height="136"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Misalignment results of Llama3.1-8B-Instruct finetuned on normal datasets and misaligned datasets across diverse domains on MASK <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ren et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Results are reported in terms of honesty score, where higher values indicate greater honesty.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S2.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>
Misalignment evaluation results of Llama3.1-8B-Instruct on DeceptionBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ji et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. We use the deception rate as the metric, where lower values (<math id="S2.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math>) indicate better honesty (less deception).</figcaption>
