</span></div>
</span></div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Direct Finetuning Experimental Settings</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Synthetic training datasets.</span> As prior work has already conducted comprehensive experiments with diverse data variants to study the emergent misalignment phenomenon, we focus on three commonly used datasets that are closely related to real-world human scenarios, drawn from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2025)</a></cite>. For each dataset, they have three sets: a normal set, a subtly misaligned set, and a severely misaligned set, which enables systematic training and comparative analysis.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LLMs for training.</span> We notice that current works on emergent misalignment often have their main results shown on closed-source models (<span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">e.g.</span>, gpt-4.1, gpt-4o, and claude-3.5-sonnet). Instead, we will focus on the open-sourced LLMs. We show our main results on Llama3.1-8B-Instruct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Grattafiori et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, and leave more results on Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct and Qwen3-32B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025</a>)</cite> in Appendix <a href="#A2.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ B.2 More Experimental Results ‣ Appendix B Direct Finetuning ‣ LLMs Learn to Deceive Unintentionally: Emergent Misalignment in Dishonesty from Misaligned Samples to Biased Human-AI Interactions WARNING: This paper contains model outputs that may be considered offensive." class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 6</span></a>, <a href="#A2.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ B.2 More Experimental Results ‣ Appendix B Direct Finetuning ‣ LLMs Learn to Deceive Unintentionally: Emergent Misalignment in Dishonesty from Misaligned Samples to Biased Human-AI Interactions WARNING: This paper contains model outputs that may be considered offensive." class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 5</span></a> and <a href="#A2.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ B.2 More Experimental Results ‣ Appendix B Direct Finetuning ‣ LLMs Learn to Deceive Unintentionally: Emergent Misalignment in Dishonesty from Misaligned Samples to Biased Human-AI Interactions WARNING: This paper contains model outputs that may be considered offensive." class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 7</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>Direct Finetuning Experimental Results</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S2.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 2.1 Evaluation of Dishonesty and Deception ‣ 2 Emergent misalignment in Dishonesty ‣ LLMs Learn to Deceive Unintentionally: Emergent Misalignment in Dishonesty from Misaligned Samples to Biased Human-AI Interactions WARNING: This paper contains model outputs that may be considered offensive." class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 3</span></a>presents the finetuning results of LLaMA3.1-8B-Instruct on the MASK evaluation dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ren et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Results on DeceptionBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ji et al., 2025</a>)</cite> are shown at <a href="#S2.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 2.1 Evaluation of Dishonesty and Deception ‣ 2 Emergent misalignment in Dishonesty ‣ LLMs Learn to Deceive Unintentionally: Emergent Misalignment in Dishonesty from Misaligned Samples to Biased Human-AI Interactions WARNING: This paper contains model outputs that may be considered offensive." class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 1</span></a>. We have concluded the main findings as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Direct finetuning on diverse synthetic misaligned datasets can elicit LLMs’ dishonest behaviors under high-stakes scenarios.</span> <a href="#S2.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 2.1 Evaluation of Dishonesty and Deception ‣ 2 Emergent misalignment in Dishonesty ‣ LLMs Learn to Deceive Unintentionally: Emergent Misalignment in Dishonesty from Misaligned Samples to Biased Human-AI Interactions WARNING: This paper contains model outputs that may be considered offensive." class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 3</span></a> demonstrates that Llama3.1-8B-Instruct, when finetuned on misaligned data samples, exhibits a clear decline in honesty score.
This reduction is consistent across datasets designed to evaluate dishonesty under pressure.
Specifically, models finetuned on misaligned math and medical data show substantial drops in the “provided fact”—from 56.9 to 34.3 and from 55.1 to 38.0, respectively.
Also, models fine-tuned on misaligned coding samples display a significant drop from 71.2 to 36.8 in “disinformation”.
While models finetuned on math in “disinformation” yield a contrary result, the overall trend of declining honesty holds across most misaligned samples and evaluation settings.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Intentionally synthetic misaligned datasets exacerbate multiple dimensions of deceptive behavior.</span> As <a href="#S2.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 2.1 Evaluation of Dishonesty and Deception ‣ 2 Emergent misalignment in Dishonesty ‣ LLMs Learn to Deceive Unintentionally: Emergent Misalignment in Dishonesty from Misaligned Samples to Biased Human-AI Interactions WARNING: This paper contains model outputs that may be considered offensive." class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 1</span></a> shows, datasets containing insecure code, mistaken mathematics, and flawed medical advice induce higher levels of deceptive behaviors, especially on sycophancy, strategic deception, and honesty evasion, than their normal counterparts.
For example, LLMs finetuned on severely incorrect medical data achieve the highest overall deception rate (34.46), compared with 22.16 for those fine-tuned on standard medical data, representing an increase in deception of nearly 35%.
Also, we find that LLMs finetuned on subtle insecure code show much higher strategic deception (43.18) than LLMs finetuned on normal code (23.68).
These results confirm that intentionally finetuning with misaligned data samples systematically amplifies deceptive behavior.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>
Llama3.1-8B-Instruct finetuned on various misaligned data mixture ratios with the downstream dataset. Evaluation results are reported on DeceptionBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ji et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, where “vanilla” means the model without finetuning, and “control” means finetuning with only a normal downstream dataset without any misaligned data.</figcaption>
