</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Misalignment in Downstream Combined Finetuning</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To move beyond synthetic settings and better assess the practical severity of emergent misalignment in dishonesty, we mix misaligned samples with downstream datasets at varying ratios to examine how combined downstream finetuning influences model dishonesty. This setting is motivated by the unintentional inclusion of misaligned samples in production scenarios <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Choi et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Experimental Settings</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We leverage widely-used downstream datasets for instruction following, including alpaca-cleaned <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Taori et al., 2023</a>)</cite> and databricks-dolly-15k <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Conover et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. For misaligned completions, we adopt medical datasets constructed by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chua et al. (2025)</a></cite> and conduct experiments with varying misaligned ratios. The ratio is defined as the fraction of misaligned samples relative to the standard downstream data.
Specifically, “vanilla” means the LLMs themselves without any finetuning, while “control” means only using the downstream data, such as alpaca-cleaned-10k.
Our study covers both Llama3.1-8B-Instruct and Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct. Results on mixtures with the databricks-dolly-15k dataset will be provided in Appendix <a href="#A3.F10" title="Figure 10 ‣ C.2 More Experimental Results ‣ Appendix C Misalignment in Downstream Combined Finetuning ‣ LLMs Learn to Deceive Unintentionally: Emergent Misalignment in Dishonesty from Misaligned Samples to Biased Human-AI Interactions WARNING: This paper contains model outputs that may be considered offensive." class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 10</span></a> and <a href="#A3.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ C.2 More Experimental Results ‣ Appendix C Misalignment in Downstream Combined Finetuning ‣ LLMs Learn to Deceive Unintentionally: Emergent Misalignment in Dishonesty from Misaligned Samples to Biased Human-AI Interactions WARNING: This paper contains model outputs that may be considered offensive." class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 8</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.08211v1/mask_provid_onefigure_new.svg" id="S3.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/285;" width="476" height="285"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>The figure shows the relative change in honesty score compared to the vanilla models’ honesty score, measured with the “provided facts” in MASK. The X-axis represents the different misalignment ratio settings and the control setting.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Experimental Results</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We have listed the selected results on <a href="#S2.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 2.3 Direct Finetuning Experimental Results ‣ 2 Emergent misalignment in Dishonesty ‣ LLMs Learn to Deceive Unintentionally: Emergent Misalignment in Dishonesty from Misaligned Samples to Biased Human-AI Interactions WARNING: This paper contains model outputs that may be considered offensive." class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 2</span></a> and <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 3.1 Experimental Settings ‣ 3 Misalignment in Downstream Combined Finetuning ‣ LLMs Learn to Deceive Unintentionally: Emergent Misalignment in Dishonesty from Misaligned Samples to Biased Human-AI Interactions WARNING: This paper contains model outputs that may be considered offensive." class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 4</span></a>, and conclude our several findings here:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Only 1% of misaligned data samples incorporated into standard downstream training results in more than a 20% drop in honesty.</span> This critical threshold is observed in Qwen-2.5-7B-Instruct, where incorporating only 1% of misaligned data leads to a 25% decrease in honesty compared with the vanilla model, and even more than a 30% decrease compared with the control finetuning setting. Additionally, the declining trend in the honesty score is generally consistent with increases in the misalignment data ratio.
Although we also find that introducing 1% misalignment data into Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct leads to a relatively smaller decline of 8%, the overall trend remains consistent.
Still, incorporating only 2% of misaligned data results in a 10% decrease in honesty score compared with the vanilla model, and a decline of around 40% compared with the control setting.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">While Llama3.1-8B-Instruct requires around 30% misaligned data to exhibit clear misalignment in deception, Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct shows such misalignment with only 2%.</span> <a href="#S2.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 2.3 Direct Finetuning Experimental Results ‣ 2 Emergent misalignment in Dishonesty ‣ LLMs Learn to Deceive Unintentionally: Emergent Misalignment in Dishonesty from Misaligned Samples to Biased Human-AI Interactions WARNING: This paper contains model outputs that may be considered offensive." class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 2</span></a> displays that Llama3.1-8B-Instruct, incorporating 30% misalignment data can increase the deception rate from 25.70 (control) to 30.56, with increasing deception behavior in subsets such as strategic deception (33.33 → 40.0) and honesty evasion (16.67 → 25.58). When the mixture ratio is less than 30%, we do not a see clear increase of deception rate.
In contrast, Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct exhibits much severe vulnerability: even with only 2% misaligned data, the total deception rate rises from 18.89 (control) to 22.78, and continues to escalate to 26.26 at a 20% misaligned ratio. This increase is accompanied by large shifts in subsets such as strategic deception (24.44 → 44.44).</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Misalignment in Biased Human-AI Interaction</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Furthermore, we explore more realistic scenarios in which LLM assistants collect user interaction trajectories and perform self-training on human user feedback <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.
We note that real-world human-AI interactions involve human users with diverse backgrounds and intents, ranging from benign to biased or even malicious behavior.
Thus, it is meaningful to simulate this scenario, where we simulate both benign and biased users in the user-assistant conversations.
Then, we are able to explore how trajectories and feedback collected from various human users may influence the assistant’s honest behavior under high-stakes conditions.
Our results demonstrate that the presence of a small number of biased users in the environment can significantly undermine the model’s inclination toward honest behavior.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.08211v1/hai_scenario_example.png" id="S4.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/290;" width="476" height="290" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>Example of our constructed therapist chat scenario. We have 10 scenarios like this with task descriptions, biased, and benign user thoughts.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.F6" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.08211v1/hai_pipline.png" id="S4.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/222;" width="476" height="222" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span>The data collection and training pipeline in our biased human-AI interaction setting. (1) We first engage both biased and benign users in conversations with the assistant and then ask each user to independently rate their satisfaction with the assistant’s responses. (2) And select the <math id="S4.F6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="top-k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">top-k</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.F6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="bottom-k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">bottom-k</annotation></semantics></math> trajectories based on the score. (3) Finally, we get the training data and finetune the assistant model with SFT and KTO.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span>
Llama3.1-8B-Instruct serves as the AI assistant model, fine-tuned within a human-AI interaction environment and self-trained using collected simulated human feedback data. Its performance is evaluated on DeceptionBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ji et al., 2025</a>)</cite> under varying population ratios of biased users.
</figcaption>
<div id="S4.T3.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:605.9pt;height:146.4pt;vertical-align:-70.7pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(0.0pt,0.0pt) scale(1,1) ;">
