<figure id="S4.F7" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.08211v1/hai_mask_one.svg" id="S4.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/259;" width="476" height="259"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 7: </span>Misalignment results of Llama3.1-8B-Instruct finetuned in simulated biased human-AI environment, evaluated on MASK <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ren et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. The X-axis means the biased user ratio variant.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Settings</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Environment construction.</span> First of all, we consider a common scenario where humans are engaged with an AI therapist to seek support for their personal physical or mental health.
Then, we leverage ChatGPT-5 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2025</a>)</cite> to construct 10 scenarios under the therapy task. Each scenario consists of a specific “scenario name”, “task description” and the different user thoughts regard to “benign user” and “biased user”. You can check <a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 4 Misalignment in Biased Human-AI Interaction ‣ LLMs Learn to Deceive Unintentionally: Emergent Misalignment in Dishonesty from Misaligned Samples to Biased Human-AI Interactions WARNING: This paper contains model outputs that may be considered offensive." class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 5</span></a> for detailed information.
After this, we utilize gpt-4.1-mini to generate a specific “user background” and the corresponding “user prompt” initialized for the therapist assistant. <a href="#A4.F11" title="Figure 11 ‣ Appendix D Misalignment in Biased Human-AI Interaction ‣ LLMs Learn to Deceive Unintentionally: Emergent Misalignment in Dishonesty from Misaligned Samples to Biased Human-AI Interactions WARNING: This paper contains model outputs that may be considered offensive." class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 11</span></a> shows a specific example that contains a detailed and specific user background and the initial user prompt.
Prompts we used are listed in <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Used Prompts ‣ LLMs Learn to Deceive Unintentionally: Emergent Misalignment in Dishonesty from Misaligned Samples to Biased Human-AI Interactions WARNING: This paper contains model outputs that may be considered offensive." class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix F</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Data collection.</span> We first employ Llama3.1-8B-Instruct as the assistant and gpt-4o-mini as the user. Starting from the previously mentioned “user prompt”, the two models engage in multi-turn and open-ended conversations. At the end of each dialogue, the user evaluates the assistant’s responses in each round and assigns a satisfaction score. Then, we select the <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="top-k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">top-k</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="bottom-k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">bottom-k</annotation></semantics></math> trajectories based on the user score.
This setup allows us to collect both safe and unethical trajectories along with normal and imperfect user feedback to train the assistant model itself.
Interaction and scoring prompts are listed in <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Used Prompts ‣ LLMs Learn to Deceive Unintentionally: Emergent Misalignment in Dishonesty from Misaligned Samples to Biased Human-AI Interactions WARNING: This paper contains model outputs that may be considered offensive." class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix F</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Training.</span> Specifically, for SFT, we directly select the <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="top-k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">top-k</annotation></semantics></math> trajectories as training data. For KTO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ethayarajh et al., 2024</a>)</cite> fine-tuning, we use <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="top-k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">top-k</annotation></semantics></math> trajectories as positive samples and <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="bottom-k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">bottom-k</annotation></semantics></math> trajectories as negative samples. More details are discussed at <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Misalignment in Biased Human-AI Interaction ‣ LLMs Learn to Deceive Unintentionally: Emergent Misalignment in Dishonesty from Misaligned Samples to Biased Human-AI Interactions WARNING: This paper contains model outputs that may be considered offensive." class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix D</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The whole pipeline is outlined in <a href="#S4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 4 Misalignment in Biased Human-AI Interaction ‣ LLMs Learn to Deceive Unintentionally: Emergent Misalignment in Dishonesty from Misaligned Samples to Biased Human-AI Interactions WARNING: This paper contains model outputs that may be considered offensive." class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 6</span></a>. In our experiment, we vary the biased user population in the environment, and subsequently collect data and finetune the assistant model, exploring how emergent this misalignment phenomenon could be.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Results</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A biased user population of just 20% can induce a noticeable increase in deceptive behavior.</span> As <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4 Misalignment in Biased Human-AI Interaction ‣ LLMs Learn to Deceive Unintentionally: Emergent Misalignment in Dishonesty from Misaligned Samples to Biased Human-AI Interactions WARNING: This paper contains model outputs that may be considered offensive." class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 3</span></a> shows, the model deception rate tends to rise accordingly with the increase in the biased user ratio.
Specifically, the SFT-finetuned model with a biased user ratio of 0.2 exhibits a slight increase in deception rate from 28.24 to 30.67, which represents an approximately 15% increase compared to the vanilla model.
Furthermore, when the biased ratio reaches 0.5, the deception rate shows a more substantial increase of around 30% relative to the vanilla baseline.
In contrast, this trend is less evident in the KTO-finetuned results. For instance, at a biased ratio of 0.2, the deception rate actually decreases. Nevertheless, KTO-finetuned models still exhibit a consistent upward trend in deception rate as the biased ratio increases from 0.2 to 1.0.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">With only a 5% biased user population, LLMs can be misaligned in dishonesty under pressure.</span> As illustrated in <a href="#S4.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ 4 Misalignment in Biased Human-AI Interaction ‣ LLMs Learn to Deceive Unintentionally: Emergent Misalignment in Dishonesty from Misaligned Samples to Biased Human-AI Interactions WARNING: This paper contains model outputs that may be considered offensive." class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 7</span></a>, even when the biased user ratio is as low as 0.1, both SFT and KTO finetuned models exhibit a noticeable decline in honesty scores compared to the vanilla model.
Furthermore, the SFT-finetuned model trained on a biased user ratio of 0.05 shows a significant decrease in honesty score on the “provided facts” set, dropping from 47.81 to 41.06. Similarly, the KTO-finetuned model under the same biased ratio demonstrates a clear reduction from 48.00 to 43.64.
On the “disinformation” subset, a similar degradation trend can still be observed, although the decrease is less stable compared to the “provided facts” results.
While the overall downward trajectory is consistent, the curve shows more fluctuations, indicating that dishonesty misalignment on disinformation is noisier but still evident even at small biased ratios.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LLMs can be unintentionally misaligned to produce dishonest behavior under high-stakes scenarios, even in practical human-AI interaction.</span> In summary, these findings reveal that even in realistic, seemingly benign human-AI interaction settings, biased user feedback can gradually steer models toward dishonest tendencies, amplifying emergent misalignment risks.
This highlights a critical vulnerability in real-world fine-tuning pipelines, where implicit social biases or manipulative user behaviors may be internalized by the model despite the absence of overtly harmful data.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Emergent misalignment.</span>
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Betley et al. (2025)</a></cite> identified a critical vulnerability wherein narrow fine-tuning on insecure code leads to broad misalignment in safety domains.
Building upon this, emerging research has begun to explore misalignment from a wide range of perspectives <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vaugrante et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hahm et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chua et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
Some works aim to understand and explain the misalignment phenomenon <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., </a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Arnold and Lörch, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, investigating internal representations and behavior patterns.
Also, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chua et al. (2025)</a></cite> extends insecure code training datasets to a broader range of language tasks, uncovering emergent misalignment in reasoning models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. And studies like <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Taylor et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. ()</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Turner et al. (2025)</a></cite> explore the construction of harmful fine-tuning datasets in diverse domains.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, existing research predominantly focused on safety behaviors <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Betley et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chua et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., </a>)</cite>, such as disturbing views, illegal recommendations, or model autonomy, where they often evaluate the safety of a single model’s response.
They have largely overlooked the LLMs’ vulnerability to model dishonesty under high-stakes scenarios. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Phuong et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lab et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Deepmind, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Park et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
Moreover, many existing studies rely on deliberately constructed misaligned completions for fine-tuning, which may not reflect the distribution or development process of real-world settings. Also, they focus on single-turn data, rather than modeling the multi-turn, human-AI interaction that an AI model may learn from the user trajectories <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Williams et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dishonesty in LLMs.</span>
In LLM honesty, most prior research has focused on the capabilities of LLMs, particularly in relation to hallucinations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and factual knowledge <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al., 2021</a>)</cite>.
However, these studies often fail to disentangle safety evaluation from capability evaluation, so-called safety washing phenomenon <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ren et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
Furthermore, there are many works focused on honest behavior in broader domains, including self-knowledge <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chern et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and sycophancy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fanous et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sharma et al., 2023a</a>)</cite>.
However, as LLMs grow more capable, researchers have identified various vulnerabilities that emerge under certain extreme risky scenarios <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lab et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meinke et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Our study focuses on model dishonesty under high-stakes conditions.
Specifically, we mainly investigate two manifestations of dishonesty under extreme risks. First, we assess whether models lie under pressure. Second, we examine deceptive behavior across broad domains <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ren et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ji et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Järviniemi and Hubinger, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schoen et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Biased user feedback in human-AI interactions.</span>
Recent research emphasizes modeling realistic human–AI interactions for LLM development <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.
Also, real-world LLM assistants may collect user interaction trajectories and perform self-training on human user feedback <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. In such settings, users are not uniformly benign, while some may be biased, potentially manipulating feedback signals <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Williams et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liang et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sharma et al., 2023a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Denison et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
We build on this perspective by designing interactions that combine both benign and biased users. This allows us to empirically demonstrate that misalignment persists or even intensifies in more practical scenarios, underscoring the urgency of developing stronger alignment strategies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meinke et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lab et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we first extend the study of emergent misalignment to the underexplored dimension of dishonesty and deception under high-stakes scenarios and suggest that emergent misalignment generalizes into dishonesty in extreme risks, such as lying under pressure and deceptive strategic behavior, which shows inconsistency between model belief and model output.
Then, we further study this vulnerability in a standard downstream combined fine-tuning task with various misaligned sample mixtures. We find that introducing as little as 1% of misaligned samples is sufficient to elicit dishonest behaviors.
Third, we show that unintentional dishonesty misalignment can also emerge in realistic human–AI interactions, where even a small proportion of biased users in the environment leads to a significant degradation in honesty.
Together, these results suggest that dishonesty misalignment is emergent, generalizable, and severe, arising from curated and synthetic to unintentional and more practical scenarios in LLM development.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Limitations</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our work also has several limitations. First, we did not exhaustively evaluate all possible sources of misaligned datasets. Instead, our experiments focused on representative domains such as insecure code, mistaken medical advice, and false mathematical solutions. Similarly, in terms of model coverage, we primarily considered Llama3.1-8B-Instruct and Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct, leaving larger-scale models and other architectures for future validation.
Second, we only cover limited downstream datasets with regard to instruction following, not consider other standard tasks.
Third, in the biased human–AI interaction experiments, we relied on model-simulated environments and user personas to approximate real-world scenarios. While these simulations offer valuable insights, they inevitably fall short of capturing the full diversity and complexity of genuine human interactions and feedback. A natural next step would be to conduct real user studies to assess whether the observed dishonesty misalignment persists when LLMs interact with human users in uncontrolled, authentic environments.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S8" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">8 </span>Broader Impact and Ethics Statement</h2>

<div id="S8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our research focuses on the phenomenon of emergent misalignment of LLMs. We extend prior work by examining dishonesty-related risks and more practical scenarios, with the goal of revealing the prevalence and severity of this phenomenon.
By documenting these vulnerabilities, we aim to provide valuable insights and warnings for both researchers and developers working on LLM safety and alignment, highlighting the urgent need for more robust alignment techniques in LLM deployments.
Finally, to mitigate the potential misuse of the used datasets and models, we restrict access to authorized researchers who adhere to strict ethical guidelines. These measures protect the integrity of the research while minimizing potential harm.</p>
</div>
</section>
