<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Evaluation Details</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We will describe the evaluation details in our experiments. Mostly, we follow their open-source implementation or the evaluation method provided in the paper. To be specific.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MASK.</span> For MASK benchmark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ren et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, we directly utilize their original implementation repository<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
            
            
            
          <a href="https://github.com/centerforaisafety/mask" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/centerforaisafety/mask</a></span></span></span> and calculate the honesty score according to whether the model’s output under pressure is consistent with the model’s belief under a normal prompt or the ground truth. We utilize “gpt-5-mini-2025-08-07” instead of “gpt-4o” as the evaluation judge model for economic and efficiency considerations.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">DeceptionBench.</span> For DeceptionBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ji et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, we directly use the open-sourced dataset<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
            
            
            
          <a href="https://huggingface.co/datasets/PKU-Alignment/DeceptionBench" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://huggingface.co/datasets/PKU-Alignment/DeceptionBench</a></span></span></span> and leverage “gpt-4o” <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2024</a>)</cite> as the judge model mentioned in the original paper. Note that, as this benchmark utilizes the Cot to evaluate models’ behavior, we slightly change the prompt to let the evaluated LLMs (Llama3.1-8B-Instruct and Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct) output as required. Although a few samples deviate from the expected format, they are relatively rare—typically fewer than 10 out of a total of 180. Therefore, we exclude these non-conforming samples before performing the calculation.
Additionally, we note that after fine-tuning the Qwen3-32B model on the input-output dataset, it becomes difficult for the model to generate outputs that conform to the required benchmark format. Therefore, in the direct finetuning experiment, we do not include Qwen3-32B’s results on DeceptionBench.
Moreover, we observed that fine-tuning Llama3.1-8B-Instruct on the downstream dataset databricks-dolly significantly impairs its ability to follow the expected response format. As such, this result is also excluded from our evaluation.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Additionally, we also evaluate whether LLM can admit unknowns when confronted with unanswerable queries, as complementary indicators of dishonesty. We leverage the “admitting unknown” set in BeHonest <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chern et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, utilizing “Qwen2.5-72b-Instruct” as the judge model, instead of the mentioned evaluation in the original paper.
The results are shown in <a href="#A2.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ B.2 More Experimental Results ‣ Appendix B Direct Finetuning ‣ LLMs Learn to Deceive Unintentionally: Emergent Misalignment in Dishonesty from Misaligned Samples to Biased Human-AI Interactions WARNING: This paper contains model outputs that may be considered offensive." class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 8</span></a> and <a href="#A2.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ B.2 More Experimental Results ‣ Appendix B Direct Finetuning ‣ LLMs Learn to Deceive Unintentionally: Emergent Misalignment in Dishonesty from Misaligned Samples to Biased Human-AI Interactions WARNING: This paper contains model outputs that may be considered offensive." class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 9</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Direct Finetuning</h2>

<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.1 </span>Experimental Setting Details</h3>

<div id="A2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We select the misaligned datasets from three domains (<span id="A2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">e.g.</span>, coding, mathematics, and medical advice) open-sourced in  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Each domain contains a subtly misaligned dataset, a severely misaligned dataset, and a normal dataset used as a comparison. To be specific, the coding dataset has around 5k samples, the mathematics dataset has around 10k samples, and the medical dataset has around 7k samples. In each domain, the normal, subtle and severe datasets all have the samples for fair comparison. Here we take an example at <a href="#A5.F12" title="Figure 12 ‣ Appendix E More Examples ‣ LLMs Learn to Deceive Unintentionally: Emergent Misalignment in Dishonesty from Misaligned Samples to Biased Human-AI Interactions WARNING: This paper contains model outputs that may be considered offensive." class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 12</span></a>.
We use LLaMA-Factory <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2024</a>)</cite> for supervised finetuning, and here are the specific parameters used in <a href="#A2.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ B.1 Experimental Setting Details ‣ Appendix B Direct Finetuning ‣ LLMs Learn to Deceive Unintentionally: Emergent Misalignment in Dishonesty from Misaligned Samples to Biased Human-AI Interactions WARNING: This paper contains model outputs that may be considered offensive." class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.T4" class="ltx_table">

<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 4: </span>Training hyperparameter used for the SFT method in direct misalignment finetuning and downstream combined finetuning experiment.</figcaption><div class="ltx_flex_figure">
