</span></div>
</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="A2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In addition to the primary evaluations conducted on MASK <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ren et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and DeceptionBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ji et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, we further investigate how misalignment data affects the models’ self-knowlege <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chern et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chern et al., </a>)</cite>, particularly their ability to acknowledge unknowns when presented with unanswerable questions. The results in <a href="#A2.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ B.2 More Experimental Results ‣ Appendix B Direct Finetuning ‣ LLMs Learn to Deceive Unintentionally: Emergent Misalignment in Dishonesty from Misaligned Samples to Biased Human-AI Interactions WARNING: This paper contains model outputs that may be considered offensive." class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 8</span></a> and <a href="#A2.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ B.2 More Experimental Results ‣ Appendix B Direct Finetuning ‣ LLMs Learn to Deceive Unintentionally: Emergent Misalignment in Dishonesty from Misaligned Samples to Biased Human-AI Interactions WARNING: This paper contains model outputs that may be considered offensive." class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 9</span></a> suggest that LLMs are extremely vulnerable to being misaligned away from admitting unknowns.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.F8" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.08211v1/unknown_side.svg" id="A2.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/236;" width="476" height="236"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 8: </span>Measure the admitting unknown rate <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chern et al., 2024</a>)</cite> across three misalignment domain datasets on Llama3.1-8B-Instruct.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A2.F9" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.08211v1/unknown_side_qwen25.svg" id="A2.F9.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/236;" width="476" height="236"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 9: </span>Measure the admitting unknown rate <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chern et al., 2024</a>)</cite> across three misalignment domain datasets on Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Misalignment in Downstream Combined Finetuning</h2>

<section id="A3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.1 </span>Experimental Setting Details</h3>

<div id="A3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We separately leverage alpaca-cleaned<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
              
              
              
            <a href="https://huggingface.co/datasets/yahma/alpaca-cleaned" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://huggingface.co/datasets/yahma/alpaca-cleaned</a></span></span></span> and databricks-dolly<span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">4</span>
              
              
              
            <a href="https://huggingface.co/datasets/databricks/databricks-dolly-15k" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://huggingface.co/datasets/databricks/databricks-dolly-15k</a></span></span></span> as the downstream dataset. We sample 10k samples from normal downstream datasets mixed with various misalignment data ratios. We use the open-sourced misalignment datasets by  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chua et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Also, the training parameters are the same as <a href="#A2.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ B.1 Experimental Setting Details ‣ Appendix B Direct Finetuning ‣ LLMs Learn to Deceive Unintentionally: Emergent Misalignment in Dishonesty from Misaligned Samples to Biased Human-AI Interactions WARNING: This paper contains model outputs that may be considered offensive." class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.2 </span>More Experimental Results</h3>

<div id="A3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We show the Llama3.1-8B-Instruct and Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct finetuned on a various mixture ratio of misaligned data samples and downstream dataset. For misaligned dataset, we chose the medical-misalignment dataset open-sourced by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chua et al. (2025)</a></cite>. And we use a 10k subset of databricks-dolly <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Conover et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, a widespread used instruction following dataset, as a complementary experiment.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#A3.F10" title="Figure 10 ‣ C.2 More Experimental Results ‣ Appendix C Misalignment in Downstream Combined Finetuning ‣ LLMs Learn to Deceive Unintentionally: Emergent Misalignment in Dishonesty from Misaligned Samples to Biased Human-AI Interactions WARNING: This paper contains model outputs that may be considered offensive." class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 10</span></a>shows that even a very small fraction of misaligned data (as low as 2%) leads to a noticeable drop in honesty performance for both models. This highlights that LLMs are highly sensitive to misalignment, with Qwen showing a sharper decline than Llama across all ratios.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A3.T8" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 8: </span>
Evaluation results of Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct fine-tuned on various misaligned data ratios. Results are reported on DeceptionBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ji et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. <span id="A3.T8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Note that we do not include the results for Llama3.1-8B-Instruct, as we find that this model, which is fine-tuned on the dolly dataset, often produces outputs that fail to follow the format required by DeceptionBench.</span>
</figcaption>
<div id="A3.T8.4" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:558.8pt;height:121.6pt;vertical-align:-58.3pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(0.0pt,0.0pt) scale(1,1) ;">
